window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][111] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A cycle in which a system's outputs become its future inputs, so its own choices shape the data it learns from.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A recommender shows popular songs, which get played, which trains it to show them more.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A model trained once on data it did not influence.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-fine-tuning\"></span>\n<h3>fine-tuning</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Further training of a model on examples of the desired behavior so that it follows a style, format or task more reliably; it does not reliably add new facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Training a small model on 10,000 labeled support tickets to classify them.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Pasting a policy into the prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-first-principles-reasoning\"></span>\n<h3>first-principles reasoning</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Breaking a problem into its basic facts and rebuilding the answer from them instead of copying an existing solution.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Pricing a delivery service by building up cost per order from courier time, fuel and platform overhead.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Copying a competitor's price list.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-first-run-experience\"></span>\n<h3>first-run experience</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> What a person sees and does from first opening a product or device until they have reached their first real value, including setup, permissions and the first task.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Unboxing earbuds, pairing them, running a fit test and starting a first listening session.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The experience of a long-time user who already knows the product.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C11</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-five-whys\"></span>\n<h3>five whys</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A root-cause technique that asks why repeatedly, usually about five times, until it reaches a cause that can be acted on.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Tracing a missed notification run back to an expired credential and then to a missing ownership process.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Stopping at the first answer, or concluding that a person made a mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-framework-theater\"></span>\n<h3>framework theater</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Naming or drawing a framework without using it to reach a conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Reciting a prioritization acronym and then ranking items by gut feel.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Scoring each option on stated criteria and choosing from the scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-funnel\"></span>\n<h3>funnel</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The ordered steps a user passes through to reach an outcome, with drop-off measured at each step.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Visit, sign up, add payment method, first purchase.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An unordered list of features and their usage.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-go-to-market\"></span>\n<h3>go-to-market</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The plan for how a product reaches its target customers and wins them: who the customer is, the problem and value proposition, positioning and message, price and packaging, channels, launch, and the metrics that show whether it is working.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A one-page plan for an expense-capture feature naming the target owners, the message, the in-product launch and the success measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A list of marketing tactics with no target or success measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-goodharts-law\"></span>\n<h3>Goodhart's law</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The observation that when a measure becomes a target it stops being a good measure, because effort shifts to the measure rather than the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Teams raising notification volume to lift a daily-active count.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A metric rising because the product genuinely improved.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-graceful-degradation\"></span>\n<h3>graceful degradation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A system's ability to keep providing a reduced but useful service when a part fails, instead of failing completely.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A product page that loads without recommendations when the recommendation service is down.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A blank page or an endless spinner when one dependency fails.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C22</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-grain\"></span>\n<h3>grain</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> What one row of a table, or of a query result, represents: one row per user, per event, per order or per user per day; most wrong counts come from not knowing the grain of the tables joined or of the result wanted.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The events table has one row per user per event, so a user with three events has three rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of columns in a table, or its total row count.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-grounding\"></span>\n<h3>grounding</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Tying a model's output to supplied sources so that its claims can be traced and checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An answer that quotes the current refund policy and links to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An answer written from the model's memory with no source.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-growth-loop\"></span>\n<h3>growth loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A closed system in which the output of one cycle becomes the input of the next, so that growth compounds, as when users invite others who then invite more.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A referral loop with 4 invitations per user and 12% conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A one-way funnel that must be refilled with paid users at the top each time.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-guardrail\"></span>\n<h3>guardrail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A control placed around a model to keep its behavior within limits, such as input and output filters, rules about what it may do, limits on tools and permissions, and escalation to people.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An output filter that blocks private account data and a cap on refund amounts.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A metric that must not worsen in an experiment, which is a guardrail metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-guardrail-metric\"></span>\n<h3>guardrail metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A metric that must not get worse while a team pursues its primary goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Crash rate, during a launch meant to make the app faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The metric the launch is trying to improve.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hallucination\"></span>\n<h3>hallucination</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A model output that is fluent but unsupported by its inputs or by fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A model citing a study that does not exist.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A correct answer phrased awkwardly.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-headroom\"></span>\n<h3>headroom</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The gap between how a segment performs at a journey step today and a realistic level it could reach, which bounds how much an improvement there can add.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A checkout step where 40% of users succeed and comparable products reach 70% has a lot of headroom.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A step where 95% of people already succeed, however annoying it is to the other 5%.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C08</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hippo\"></span>\n<h3>HiPPO</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The highest-paid person's opinion, deciding by seniority instead of an evidence rule agreed in advance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A roadmap set in a meeting that reverses when a senior person changes their mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A decision made by a rule agreed before a test, with a senior person committing to the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C29</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hiring-committee\"></span>\n<h3>hiring committee</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A group, separate from the interviewers, that reviews written interview feedback and decides whether to hire.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A panel that reads each interviewer's written evidence about a candidate it never met.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A hiring manager deciding alone after the interviews.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-holdout\"></span>\n<h3>holdout</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A group deliberately kept from a change (or a set of changes) for a period so that its outcome serves as the comparison, often over a long horizon.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A 2% group of members kept on the old experience for months to see whether a quarter's launches added up.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A group left out only because it was hard to reach.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-human-in-the-loop\"></span>\n<h3>human in the loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A design in which a person reviews or approves an AI output or action before it takes effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The user confirms an order summary before an assistant places it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An agent that sends emails with no review.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-hypothesis\"></span>\n<h3>hypothesis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A specific, testable statement about a cause or an effect that could turn out to be false.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;The drop comes from Android users on the latest release.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Something is wrong with Android.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-idempotency\"></span>\n<h3>idempotency</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The property that doing an operation more than once has the same effect as doing it once; an idempotency key lets a server recognize a retried request and return the first result instead of repeating the action.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A retried 'charge $50' request returns the original confirmation and charges the card once.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A retry that charges the customer $100.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-influence-without-authority\"></span>\n<h3>influence without authority</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Getting people to act on a proposal when you are not their manager and cannot require it, by understanding their incentives, finding a shared goal, bringing evidence, pre-wiring and making the ask easy.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Securing engineering support by showing the shared goal, trading scope and asking for a half-day estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Pressure, false urgency or hiding the costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C32</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-information-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>information architecture</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The way content and functions are organized, grouped and labeled so people can find them.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Deciding that notification controls sit under a clearly named Settings group with a search box.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The visual style of the screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-input-metric\"></span>\n<h3>input metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A metric a team can move directly that drives the north star.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Share of new teams that invite a colleague in week one.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The north star itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-instrumentation\"></span>\n<h3>instrumentation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The code that records user actions and system events as data, which is how a metric becomes a number.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An invite_sent event with its time, user and channel.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A dashboard that displays numbers computed from events.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-inter-rater-agreement\"></span>\n<h3>inter-rater agreement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The extent to which independent reviewers give the same judgment, corrected for the agreement expected by chance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 94% raw agreement that is a kappa of 0.63 once chance is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Raw percent agreement on a set where nearly every item is good.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-interference\"></span>\n<h3>interference</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A situation in which one unit's treatment affects another unit's outcome, breaking independence between groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> In a marketplace, discounts for buyers in the test group change prices for buyers in the control group.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A feature that only affects the user who sees it.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-internal-platform\"></span>\n<h3>internal platform</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A product, such as a service, tool or set of interfaces, whose customers are other teams within the same company, who cannot easily be forced to be delighted and adopt it when it saves them time or risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A shared experimentation service used by forty product teams.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A consumer product sold to the public.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-interview-loop\"></span>\n<h3>interview loop</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The set of interviews a candidate completes in one hiring process, usually in a single day or a few days, whose feedback is combined into one decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Four 45-minute interviews on product design, metrics, a technical conversation and behavioral questions, followed by a debrief.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A single phone call with a recruiter.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C33</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-inversion\"></span>\n<h3>inversion</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Solving a problem by asking what would guarantee failure and then avoiding it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Listing how to make users mute every notification, then designing the opposite.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Brainstorming features that might delight users.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-issue-tree\"></span>\n<h3>issue tree</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A hierarchy that breaks a question into MECE branches, down to items that can be tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Revenue drop split into traffic, conversion and revenue per order, then each split further.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A flat list of ideas with no hierarchy.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-jobs-to-be-done\"></span>\n<h3>jobs to be done</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A lens that describes the progress a user is trying to make in a situation, which the product is hired to help with.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;Help me arrive at a meeting on time without stress.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Users aged 25 to 34.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C06</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-kill-criteria\"></span>\n<h3>kill criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Conditions, set in advance of a test or project, under which the team agrees to stop; written first to prevent moving the goalposts.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Stop if week-8 retention is below 8% or fewer than 30% of surveyed users would be very disappointed without the product.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A decision to stop made after seeing results and chosen to justify an existing preference.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C26</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-know-your-customer\"></span>\n<h3>know your customer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The process of verifying the identity of a customer, and for businesses their ownership and activity, required by financial regulation along with related monitoring obligations.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An identity and address check before a payments account is enabled.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A marketing survey about customer preferences.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-label\"></span>\n<h3>label</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The outcome a model is trying to predict for each training example.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Marked as spam; watched to the end; later disputed as fraud.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An input attribute such as the time of day.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-lagging-indicator\"></span>\n<h3>lagging indicator</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A measure that reflects results after they have happened.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Quarterly churn.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Number of onboarding steps completed on day one.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-latency\"></span>\n<h3>latency</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The time between a request and its response.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The delay between tapping search and seeing results.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> How many requests a system can handle per second.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-leading-indicator\"></span>\n<h3>leading indicator</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A measure that moves before the outcome it predicts.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> First-week activity, as a predictor of 90-day retention.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Last quarter's revenue.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-legacy-user\"></span>\n<h3>legacy user</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A user or customer who depends on the current behavior of a product (habits, integrations, stored data or contracts), so that a change creates cost for them even when it benefits others.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An enterprise customer whose weekly reporting scripts read the current export columns.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A brand-new user with no habits or integrations to break.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C08</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-lifetime-value\"></span>\n<h3>lifetime value</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The total contribution a customer is expected to generate over their life with the product; in a simple model, contribution per month divided by monthly churn. Abbreviated LTV.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> $6 a month of contribution at 5% monthly churn gives a lifetime value of $120.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Total revenue to date from a customer.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-liquidity\"></span>\n<h3>liquidity</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> In a marketplace, the likelihood that a participant on one side finds what they want from the other side quickly; measured by the share of searches with an available relevant result, conversion given a result and supplier utilization.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 40% of searches in a city show an available listing and hosts book 0.25 times a week.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of sellers who have signed up.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-llm\"></span>\n<h3>large language model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A model trained on large amounts of text to predict the next token, which can generate and transform language.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A model that drafts an email reply from a prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A database that returns stored answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C24</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-local-optimum\"></span>\n<h3>local optimum</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The best result reachable by small changes from the current position, which may be far worse than a result reachable by a different approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A signup flow tuned by copy tests to a plateau while a different first screen would double conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The best possible result across all approaches.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-localization\"></span>\n<h3>localization</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Adapting a product to a market's language, norms, infrastructure and rules so that it works and feels right there; translation is only its first layer.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Showing a fare upfront, accepting cash and letting riders describe a pickup by landmark when street numbers are not used.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Translating the interface text and leaving the sign-up, payment and address flows unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C09</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mece\"></span>\n<h3>MECE</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A way of splitting a problem into parts that do not overlap and together cover the whole.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Splitting signups by platform: iOS, Android, web.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Splitting signups into &quot;mobile&quot;, &quot;iOS&quot; and &quot;new users&quot;, which overlap and leave gaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-message-queue\"></span>\n<h3>message queue</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A holding area for tasks or messages that workers process later, so the part of the system that receives a request need not wait for slow work.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A confirmation email waiting in a queue while the user already sees a quick response.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A function that blocks until the email is sent.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-metric\"></span>\n<h3>metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A defined quantity, measured over a stated population and time window, that tracks one aspect of product performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;Share of new users who complete a first purchase within seven days.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Engagement.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-metric-diagnosis\"></span>\n<h3>metric diagnosis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A structured investigation into why a metric changed, ending in a cause, a decision and a communication.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Confirming a signup drop is real, isolating it to one platform and one release, and recommending a rollback.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Guessing a cause and proposing a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-metric-tree\"></span>\n<h3>metric tree</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A diagram that decomposes a metric into components that multiply or add up to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Orders = visitors × conversion rate; each factor then decomposed further.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A dashboard of unrelated metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-minimum-detectable-effect\"></span>\n<h3>minimum detectable effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The smallest change an experiment is designed to detect reliably.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Sizing a test to detect a one-point lift in conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The change the team hopes for.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mix-shift\"></span>\n<h3>mix shift</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A change in an overall metric caused by a change in the composition of users or traffic rather than a change in behavior within groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Average order value falls because more new, low-spend users joined.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Average order value falling because prices were cut.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C13</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-moat\"></span>\n<h3>moat</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A durable advantage that protects a business from competitors.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Accumulated data that makes recommendations better with each user.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Being first to launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mock-interview\"></span>\n<h3>mock interview</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A practice interview run under realistic conditions (the real format, time limit and tools, with no pausing or hints) and scored afterward against a rubric.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A timed 45-minute product round with a partner who probes three levels deep and scores independently.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Rereading a chapter or watching a recorded sample interview.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C34</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-model\"></span>\n<h3>model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A function fitted to data to predict something about new cases, such as whether a message is spam or which items a person will value.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A fraud model that gives each payment a risk score.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A hand-written rule such as hold any payment above $10,000 from a new account.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-model-card\"></span>\n<h3>model card</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A short document stating a model's intended use, data, evaluation results, limitations and risks.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A page describing a summarizer's languages, tested error rates and known failure cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A marketing description of a model's capabilities.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-model-graded-eval\"></span>\n<h3>model-graded eval</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> An evaluation in which a language model scores another model's outputs against a rubric; fast and cheap, and it must be calibrated against human judgments because it can miss real failures.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A grader model that flags 80 of 1,000 outputs and catches 70 of the 100 that humans found bad.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Human reviewers scoring outputs against the same rubric.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C25</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-multi-homing\"></span>\n<h3>multi-homing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Participants using several platforms at the same time, which weakens lock-in and network-effect advantages.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A driver running two ride-hailing apps at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Users who can only reach their friends on one app.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-multiple-comparisons\"></span>\n<h3>multiple comparisons</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The inflation of false-positive chances when many tests or segments are examined and the most striking result is reported.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Slicing a result 20 ways at a 5% threshold gives about a 64% chance of at least one significant segment when nothing is going on.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A single comparison planned in advance.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-mvp\"></span>\n<h3>minimum viable product</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The smallest thing that can test the riskiest assumption with real users.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A manual concierge service run by hand to test demand before building software.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A full product with a few features removed for time.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-net-revenue-retention\"></span>\n<h3>net revenue retention</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The revenue from a cohort of customers a year later, including expansion and net of churn and downgrades, as a percentage of their revenue at the start; above 100 percent means existing customers grow without new customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A cohort paying $1,000,000 that pays $1,100,000 a year later has NRR of 110%.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The share of customers who did not cancel.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-network-effect\"></span>\n<h3>network effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A property in which each additional user increases the product's value to the other users.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A messaging app becomes more useful as more of a person's contacts join.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A product that is cheaper at higher volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-non-goal\"></span>\n<h3>non-goal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Something explicitly declared out of scope for a project.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;This release will not support offline mode.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A requirement postponed without saying so.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-north-star-metric\"></span>\n<h3>north star metric</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The single metric that best captures the value users get from a product and that predicts long-term success.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Weekly active teams that send messages, for a team chat product.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Page views.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C05</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-novelty-effect\"></span>\n<h3>novelty effect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A temporary change in behavior because something is new, which fades as the novelty wears off; the mirror image, a temporary dip from change aversion, is handled the same way.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A redesigned row lifts viewing by 6% in week one and settles near 1% by week five.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A stable lift that is the same in week one and week six.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-nudge\"></span>\n<h3>nudge</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A design choice that steers behavior toward an option without forbidding others or changing incentives sharply.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An unchecked, clearly worded opt-in asked at a moment when the value is clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A pre-checked box that signs people up for charges and hides the way out.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C10</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-offline-evaluation\"></span>\n<h3>offline evaluation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Judging a model on historical data without showing it to users; fast and cheap and unable to show how users react to new results.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Measuring the error of predicted ratings on held-out past ratings.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> An online experiment with real users.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-okr\"></span>\n<h3>OKR</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A goal-setting method pairing a qualitative objective with a small number of measurable key results that are outcomes, not tasks.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Objective: new users reach first success quickly. Key result: raise the share completing the first task within 10 minutes from 38% to 55%.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Key results that read 'launch the tutorial' and 'complete the redesign.'</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-on-device-processing\"></span>\n<h3>on-device processing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Running computation on the user's own device instead of sending data to a server.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Recognizing a voice command on the phone without sending the audio away.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Uploading audio to a server for transcription.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C21</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-opportunity-cost\"></span>\n<h3>opportunity cost</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value of the best alternative given up when a choice is made.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Building the custom feature displaces a planned item expected to return $1,200,000 a year.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The cost of the chosen option measured on its own.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-order-of-magnitude\"></span>\n<h3>order of magnitude</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A factor of ten, used to judge whether a quantity is about a thousand, a million or a billion.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Two estimates that differ by a factor of one hundred signal a unit error or a missing factor.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The third significant digit of an answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C04</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-overfitting\"></span>\n<h3>overfitting</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A model learning the quirks of its training data so closely that it predicts new cases poorly.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 99% accuracy on training data and 80% on held-out data.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A model that performs about equally on training and held-out data.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-p-value\"></span>\n<h3>p-value</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The probability of seeing a result at least this extreme if there were no true effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A p-value of 0.03 means data this extreme would be rare if the change did nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The probability that the change works.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-packaging\"></span>\n<h3>packaging</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The decision about what is included in each plan or tier, and how plans are named and fenced, so that different customers choose the plan that fits them.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An entry plan, a main plan and a premium plan with different limits and features.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The price of a single plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-pain-point\"></span>\n<h3>pain point</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A specific, observable difficulty a user meets while trying to do something, which can be ranked by how often it occurs and how much it costs them.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Going out too fast in the first third of a long run and fading by the last third.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> &quot;Running is hard,&quot; which names no moment and cannot be watched.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C07</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-pareto-principle\"></span>\n<h3>Pareto principle</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The pattern that a small share of causes usually produces a large share of the effects, so effort should go first to the largest few.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> One funnel step accounting for 25 of the 62 lost installs per 100.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A guarantee that exactly 20% of causes produce 80% of effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C03</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-payback-period\"></span>\n<h3>payback period</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The time it takes for a customer's contribution to repay the cost of acquiring them; acquisition cost divided by contribution per month.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> $45 of acquisition cost and $6 of monthly contribution give a payback of 7.5 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The time until a customer cancels.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C20</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-peeking\"></span>\n<h3>peeking</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Checking the results of a test repeatedly and stopping when the difference first looks significant, which inflates false positives.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Looking at ten equally spaced points and stopping at the first p below 0.05, which declares a win about 19% of the time when nothing is going on.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Watching a guardrail for harm while the test runs to its planned end.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C14</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-percentile\"></span>\n<h3>percentile</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The value below which a given share of observations fall; the 95th percentile is the value that 95% of observations do not exceed.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A page load time of 6.5 seconds at the 95th percentile means 95% of loads were that fast or faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The average load time, which can look fine while one load in twenty is very slow.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-persona\"></span>\n<h3>persona</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A composite profile of a user type, built from research, used to align a team.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A profile of a type of weekly planner, built from eight interviews and tied to how that person decides what to order.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A stock photo with a name and a hobby, invented in a workshop.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C06</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-pivot\"></span>\n<h3>pivot</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A change in a core element of the strategy (the customer, the problem, the solution, the channel or the business model) based on what was learned, while keeping what is working; a pivot is itself a hypothesis to be tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Repositioning a habit-tracking app for freelancers after one segment showed strong pull.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Abandoning the product, or making a small feature change.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C26</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-platform\"></span>\n<h3>platform</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A product that others build on or transact through, creating value for more than one group of participants.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> An app store connecting developers and users.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A product with an export button.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C19</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-popularity-bias\"></span>\n<h3>popularity bias</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A tendency for a recommender to favor already-popular items because they have the most data, which starves new and niche items of exposure and makes the popular more popular.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The top 50 songs rising from 40% to 62% of plays.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A catalog where exposure follows quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-position-bias\"></span>\n<h3>position bias</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The tendency of items shown higher in a list to be clicked more regardless of their quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> The same item gets 10% clicks at position 1 and 4% at position 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A genuine difference in how much people like two items.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-positioning\"></span>\n<h3>positioning</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The place a product occupies in customers' minds relative to the alternatives.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> &quot;The fastest option for short urban deliveries.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A tagline that does not say who the product is for or against whom.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C18</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-postmortem\"></span>\n<h3>postmortem</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A blameless review after an incident that records the timeline, causes, what went well and badly, and actions with owners.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A written review of a four-hour login outage ending in three owned actions with dates.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A search for the person who made the mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C17</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-practical-significance\"></span>\n<h3>practical significance</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Whether an effect is large enough to matter for the decision, regardless of whether it is statistically significant.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A 0.1% lift that is significant but too small to cover the cost of shipping.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A p-value below 0.05.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C12</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-prd\"></span>\n<h3>product requirements document</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A written spec of the problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, metrics and risks that aligns a team on what to build.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A three-page doc defining the problem, the success metric, and what is out of scope.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A list of feature names.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C16</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-pre-mortem\"></span>\n<h3>pre-mortem</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> A planning exercise in which a team imagines that a plan has already failed and lists the reasons, so risks are found before commitment.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Before launch, each team member writes why the feature was pulled six months later.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> A review held after a failure to assign blame.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C02</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-precision-recall\"></span>\n<h3>precision and recall</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Two measures of a classifier: precision is the share of flagged items that are correct, and recall is the share of all true items that were flagged; raising one often lowers the other.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> A spam filter that flags almost nothing has high precision and low recall.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Overall accuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C23</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-prevalence\"></span>\n<h3>prevalence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> The estimated share of views, or of content, that violates a policy, measured by sampling and review; it measures what users actually see, unlike counts of removals.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> 0.15% of views are of violating content.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> The number of items removed this week.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C27</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-prioritization\"></span>\n<h3>prioritization</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> Choosing what to do first under limited capacity, using stated criteria and acknowledged tradeoffs.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> Ranking three projects by expected impact on a goal metric against effort and risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-example:</strong> Working through requests in the order they arrived.</li>\n<li><strong>Owning chapter:</strong> C15</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"gl-problem-statement\"></span>\n<h3>problem statement</h3>\n";
